  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.347,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ This identity can be used to find how products change when the numbers being multiplied are increased or decreased by any amount. Can you see how this identity can be used when one or both numbers are decreased?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.148,
            0.847,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For example, let us reconsider the case when one number is increased by 1 and the other decreased by 1. Let us write the product "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left(a+1\\right)\\left(b-1\\right)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left(a+1\\right)\\left(b+\\left(-1\\right)\\right."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Taking m = 1 and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n = - 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in Identity 1, we have"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.209,
            0.726,
            0.228
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "ab+(1)\\times b+a\\times(-1)+(1)\\times(-1)=ab+b-a-1,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.237,
            0.662,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "which is the same expression that we obtain earlier."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.257,
            0.668,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Use Identity 1 to find how the product changes when"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.282,
            0.828,
            0.303
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) one number is decreased by 2 and the other increased by 3;"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.307,
            0.808,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) both numbers are decreased, one by 3 and the other by 4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.336,
            0.848,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the subtractions to additions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.38,
            0.825,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Generalising this, we can find the product (a + u) (b – v) as follows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.335,
            0.404,
            0.664,
            0.483
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\left( a + u \\right) \\left( b - v \\right) &= \\left( a + u \\right) b - \\left( a + u \\right) v \\\\&= ab + ub - \\left( a v + u v \\right) \\\\&= ab + ub - a v - u v.\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.488,
            0.792,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Check that this is the same as taking m = u and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n = - v"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in Identity 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.508,
            0.848,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As in Identity 1, the product (a + u) (b – v) is the sum of the product of each term of a + u (a and u) with each term of b – v (b and (–v)). Notice that the signs of the terms in the products can be determined using the usual rules of integer multiplication."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.592,
            0.187,
            0.647
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.101,
                0.592,
                0.187,
                0.647
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_13.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.602,
            0.825,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "See how the rules of integer multiplication allows us to handle multiple cases using a single identity!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.665,
            0.553,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Expand (i) (a – u) (b + v), (ii) (a – u) (b – v)."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.69,
            0.256,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We get"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.714,
            0.698,
            0.733
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_17.jpg",
          "content": "( a - u ) ( b + v ) = a b - u b + a v - u v , { \\mathrm { a n d } }"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.744,
            0.655,
            0.763
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_18.jpg",
          "content": "( a - u ) \\; ( b - v ) = a b - u b - a v + u v ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.768,
            0.846,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The distributive property is not restricted to two terms within a bracket."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.798,
            0.473,
            0.824
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Example 1: Expand "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\textstyle { \\frac { 3 a } { 2 } }   ( a - b + { \\frac { 1 } { 5 } } )"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.3,
            0.826,
            0.696,
            0.855
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_21.jpg",
          "content": "{ \\frac { 3 a } { 2 } } ( a - \\mathbf { b } + { \\frac { 1 } { 5 } } ) = ( { \\frac { 3 a } { 2 } } \\times a ) - ( { \\frac { 3 a } { 2 } } \\times b ) + ( { \\frac { 3 a } { 2 } } \\times { \\frac { 1 } { 5 } } ) ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.859,
            0.582,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The terms can be simplified as follows—"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.409,
            0.881,
            0.588,
            0.909
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_23.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{3a}{2} \\times a = \\frac{3}{2} \\times (a \\times a)."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.079,
            0.915,
            0.126,
            0.936
          ],
